  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nWarm/dry relatively pleasant conditions look to continue through\nFriday. On Saturday a digging shortwave trough will meet up with\nthe remnants of tropical cyclone Isaias. The outflow from \nIsaias appears to influence the shape of the trough and \nassociated jet max as it squeezes into the south and becomes \nsomewhat negatively tilted. By Saturday morning the center of \nthe cyclone is projected to be over AL/MS with a sharp trough \njust to our southwest and an 80-100 kt jet maxima evacuating \nmass from the storm and its vicinity. This should lead to a \ntremendous amount of large scale ascent with the injection of \ntropical moisture. The track of the system has sped up since \nyesterday with the probability of rainfall increasing quite a \nbit on Saturday compared to 24 hours ago with the system \nappearing more likely to be clearing to the east by Sunday \nafternoon/evening. \n\nThe model consensus is pretty good on a hurricane coming onshore\nin line with the NHC track Saturday morning. The storm moves\nquickly into the mid-south and undergoes extratropical\ntransition owing to the abundant large scale ascent. The\ndeterministic GFS/ECMWF wind fields, particularly at 850/925 mb\nremain quite strong although a relatively stable boundary layer\nmay limit mixing even during the afternoon due to cloud cover.\nThere does appear to be at least some potential for advisory\nlevel gusts (40 mph or higher) - with the probability for that\nprobably in the 20-30% range. The stronger lower level wind\nfield though does drive a lot of warm air advection and\nisentropic lift, and if they are as strong as the 12z ECMWF\nindicates rainfall would likely be heavier than current QPF\nindicates. I'm a little concerned the GFS spins the strength of\nthe system down too fast and the ECMWF may be on the right\ntrack. This would lead to a little more of a heavy rain/flooding\nthreat. The severe risk/tornado risk appears muted due to\nlimited boundary layer instability but the shear appears strong\nenough for it to at least potentially be a concern. The risk\nprobabilities are higher to the south and southeast of the area\nbut likely not quite zero, especially over western Kentucky.\n\nA backdoor cold front tries to work through on Tuesday before\nsoutherly return flow turns back up and keeps things fairly\nwarm, slightly above normal, through the remainder of the week.",
